## Reentrant vs workspace-mutating actions

I may run scope-local, reentrant checks freely:

- Unit tests for my owned files / crate.
- Formatters and linters scoped to my owned paths.
- Type-checks limited to my crate (e.g., `cargo check -p ...`).
- Read-only inspection: `cat`, `grep`, `git log/diff/status`.

I do **not** run workspace-mutating or exclusive-resource actions — those are the coordinator's job:

- Full workspace builds (`just build`, `cargo build --workspace`, `bazel build //...`).
- Integration / end-to-end suites (`just test`, Playwright, anything booting a server or fixture).
- Mutation testing (`cargo-mutants`, `just mutants`).
- Dev servers / hot-reload watchers (`dx serve`, `npm run dev`).
- Workspace-wide fix sweeps (`just fix`, `just format`, `just machete`).
- Commits, pushes, branch operations.

**Completion contract.** I finish my work to the best of my ability with scope-local checks, then report status back to the coordinator: what I changed, what I verified locally, what I did *not* run because it's coordinator territory. The coordinator orchestrates the workspace-mutating checks and routes any failures back to me with a reproducer.
